search_sources — evaluation
============================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: search_sources (plans/search_sources)

task
----
find WHERE in the library something lives when its name is unknown —
the discovery bridge: from a concept to a theory long name that the
other tools can then act on.

setup
-----
fresh server.

prompt
------
"Somewhere in this Isabelle installation the library defines a
'sorted list of a multiset' operation (turning a multiset into a
sorted list). I don't know what it's called or where it lives. Find
the theory that defines it and the exact name of the operation, and
show me the defining lines."

success
-------
sorted_list_of_multiset located in HOL-Library's Multiset theory,
with the theory long name and real source lines in the answer
(readable via the theory resource or the snippet itself).

signals
-------
- query formulation: regex correctness first call (plans/
  search_sources pinned: invalid regex is an ERROR, no silent
  literal fallback — this eval measures how often that error fires
  in practice and whether its text produces a fixed retry).
- search strategy: sensible term progression ("sorted_list",
  "multiset.*sorted") vs natural-language queries pasted as regex
  (the give-away that the description didn't say "regex" loudly
  enough).
- truncation handling: a broad first query will hit the cap — does
  the truncation note lead to narrowing, or does the agent answer
  from a truncated list as if complete?
- the handoff: from a hit's theory long name, does the agent reach
  the source lines (theory resource with ?lines=, or the snippet)?
  the discovery chain's last link.

reflection
----------
- if natural-language-as-regex dominates, either say "regular
  expression" first word in the parameter description or accept
  plain substring by default with an explicit regex flag (spec
  change — cheaper for agents, this eval legitimates it).
